<Layout
title="How I work — Mike Trounce"
description="The short version, and the more technical version, of how I actually work with founders."
>
<div class="options-page">
<a href="/" class="back-link">&larr; Mike Trounce</a>

<h1>How I work</h1>

<section class="hiw-section">
<h2>The short version</h2>
<p>
We sit down and have a chat — over a coffee, on the phone, or on a video call. That's genuinely most of it. Some conversations take a few minutes; if there's real complexity to a project, it can run closer to an hour. Either way, that conversation is where the actual information comes from.
</p>
<p>
Everything else — the website, the flyer, the accounts, whatever needs doing — comes out of what we talk about there.
</p>
</section>

<p class="tech-note">
Everything below this gets a bit more technical — how those conversations actually turn into finished work. Worth a read if you're curious, easy to skip if you're not.
</p>

<section class="hiw-section">
<h2>The technical version</h2>
<p>
I record our conversations — usually just on my phone, using an app called Wispr Flow — and the transcript goes into a private Obsidian vault I keep for your company. From that, I build a wiki that keeps updating itself as we talk more: a current source of truth for what's actually going on, rather than something that goes stale after one meeting.
</p>
<p>
It's most useful once a project has real complexity — several people involved, moving parts, decisions made over weeks rather than in one sitting — but it earns its keep on smaller projects too, since it means I never have to ask you to repeat yourself.
</p>
<p>
Depending on what suits you, you can get access to the live wiki itself, or a summary document or web page pulled from it — whichever's more useful to you.
</p>
<p>
Most of the actual work — turning a conversation into a working site, a fixed flyer, a tidied set of accounts — I do with AI. I run a Claude Pro subscription (£20 a month), usually on Sonnet 5 at medium effort, and OpenCode running Qwen 3.8 Max for extra capacity when Claude Pro's quiet. All of it runs off a Mac Mini M4 and an iPhone, with a separate Obsidian vault for each company I work with.
</p>
<p>
One thing worth being upfront about: people naturally aren't perfectly consistent between conversations — a detail gets remembered differently, something genuinely changes, or two things you've told me just don't quite line up. Part of what the wiki does is surface those inconsistencies rather than quietly picking one version and moving on, so we can sort out what's actually true together, in a friendly way.
</p>
<p>
It's close to what Andrej Karpathy has described as a living, constantly-updating document, rather than a one-off report that goes stale the moment it's written.
</p>
</section>
</div>
</Layout>

